-- ============================================================================
-- 36i (2026-06-15): THE FAITHFULNESS VERDICT — does coercing a ParaType-5 PVR
-- Sprite into 2 affine triangles [P,C,A,B]->(P,C,A)+(A,C,B) FAITHFULLY reproduce
-- how flycast/PVR rasterizes that sprite? Answer read END-TO-END from the raster
-- path (not geometry intuition): YES, it is byte-faithful. flycast itself draws
-- the sprite as exactly those 2 perspective-correct triangles. So the 2-triangle
-- coercion is NOT why the names still garble after 3 fixes — the cause is the
-- INPUT data (stale/buggy capture), not the triangulation. feat/render-replica-live.
-- ============================================================================

UPSERT source:hud_sprite_raster_path_flycast SET kind='code',
  ref='core/hw/pvr/ta_vtx.cpp CaclulateSpritePlane (L1075-1122) + AppendSpriteVertexA/B (L1047-1153); core/hw/pvr/ta_ctx.h struct Vertex (L14-26: float x,y,z; u8 col[4]; float u,v — NO plane eqn stored); core/hw/pvr/ta_util.cpp makeIndex (L388-443, strip->idx) + sortTriangles (L66-185, autosort tr strip walk); core/rend/gles/gldraw.cpp DrawList (L288-308: glDrawElements(GL_TRIANGLE_STRIP, params->count, ...)) + drawSorted (L312-326: GL_TRIANGLES from sortedTriangles); core/rend/gles/gles.cpp vertex shader (L106 vtx_uv=vec3(in_uv,vpos.z), L119-121 vtx_uv.xy*=vpos.z) + fragment (L285 texture(tex,vtx_uv.xy) / L287 textureProj(tex,vtx_uv)).',
  note='THE END-TO-END PVR-SPRITE RASTER PATH IN FLYCAST — the authoritative basis for the faithfulness verdict. (1) EXPANSION (ta_vtx.cpp): a ParaType-5 Sprite submits 3 explicit XYZ corners (A=cv2,B=cv3,C=cv1) + 4 UVs; CaclulateSpritePlane SOLVES the 4th corner P=cv0\'s z/u/v ONCE, at parse time, as a plane interpolation (P.u=A_u+k1*AB_u+k2*AC_u, k1/k2 = P\'s coords in the A->B,A->C basis). (2) STORAGE (ta_ctx.h): the result is 4 ORDINARY Vertex records {x,y,z,col,u,v} in rc.verts — there is NO sprite flag, NO stored plane equation, NO separate sprite-UV channel. After expansion a sprite is INDISTINGUISHABLE from a 4-vert polygon strip. (3) INDEXING (ta_util.cpp): makeIndex/sortTriangles walk the 4 verts as a triangle strip -> triangles (P,C,A)+(A,C,B) (shared diagonal C-A). (4) DRAW (gldraw.cpp): glDrawElements(GL_TRIANGLE_STRIP, count=4) for op/pt, or GL_TRIANGLES over the sorted pairs for autosort tr — EITHER WAY exactly those 2 triangles. (5) RASTER (gles.cpp shader): per-vertex (u,v) is carried as vtx_uv.xy, premultiplied by vpos.z (=1/w) in the vertex shader and divided back via textureProj/`/vtx_uv.z` in the fragment shader = STANDARD perspective-correct linear interpolation of u/v across each triangle. There is NO per-pixel plane re-evaluation, NO bilinear-across-the-quad UV, NO sprite-specific raster mode. CONCLUSION: flycast rasterizes a PVR Sprite as 2 perspective-correct triangles sharing the C-A diagonal, with P\'s UV being the plane-interpolated value computed once at expansion. This is IDENTICAL to drawing a 4-vert polygon. (On real PVR2 hardware the ISP/TSP processes the sprite per-tile via the same edge/UV interpolation it uses for the second triangle of a quad; flycast\'s plane-solve reproduces the hardware\'s derived 4th-corner UV. For a planar screen-space HUD quad — all z equal, w constant — the perspective term is a no-op and the interpolation is purely affine, so affine 2-triangle == the hardware result exactly.)';

UPSERT finding:hud_sprite_2tri_coercion_is_faithful SET
  statement='FAITHFULNESS VERDICT (task 1, cited to the raster path, NOT geometry intuition): coercing a ParaType-5 PVR Sprite into 2 affine/perspective-correct triangles [P,C,A,B] -> (P,C,A)+(A,C,B) IS a BYTE-FAITHFUL reproduction of how flycast/PVR rasterizes that sprite. It is NOT a breaking approximation, and it is NOT why the names still garble. GROUNDING (source:hud_sprite_raster_path_flycast, every link a read source line): flycast does NOT keep the sprite as a single-plane primitive through raster. CaclulateSpritePlane (ta_vtx.cpp L1075) resolves the 4th corner P\'s u/v/z via the plane equation ONCE at TA-parse time; the result is stored as 4 plain Vertex records (ta_ctx.h struct Vertex has float u,v,z and NO plane data); ta_util.cpp triangulates those 4 verts as a strip into triangles (P,C,A)+(A,C,B); gldraw.cpp draws GL_TRIANGLE_STRIP / sorted GL_TRIANGLES; the gles.cpp shader does ordinary perspective-correct (u*z,v*z,z)+textureProj interpolation per triangle. So flycast\'s OWN sprite render IS 2 triangles interpolating UV affinely (perspective-correct), sharing the C-A diagonal, with P carrying the plane-interpolated UV. The client\'s identity-strip coercion of the captured 4 corners {P,C,A,B} (each with its captured u/v, P\'s being the plane UV) through the SAME 2-triangle path therefore reproduces flycast EXACTLY — pvr2-renderer.mjs _buildIndexBuffer emits the identical (P,C,A)+(A,C,B). The "single-plane UV mapping requires a native sprite render that 2 triangles cannot reproduce" hypothesis is REFUTED: there is no per-pixel single-plane stage in flycast; the plane equation is a one-time CORNER solve, after which the quad is 2 triangles. The thin/italic glyph slices are sheared parallelograms (correct geometry); 2 triangles with the right corner UVs render them exactly — a wrong DIAGONAL or a wrong corner UV is what shears them, and both are properties of the captured corner data, not of the triangulation method. WHY THE 3 FIXES FAILED: the triangulation was never the bug. (a) The first fixes (36c/36d) corrected the para=4 strip order [0,1,3,2]->[0,1,2,3] — right for polygons, irrelevant to the para=5 name sprites. (b) The sprite-aware centroid/guard fix (36g) and the de-heuristic identity fix (36h) both used [P,C,A,B] correctly, but were validated/run against the OLD-SERVER capture (_hud_cap_namegap) in which collectHudQuads\' rc.idx walk MIS-CAPTURED 46/62 sprite quads (finding:hud_sprite_capture_server_bug) — merged/collapsed corners that streak under ANY faithful triangulation because the INPUT corners are wrong. The 2-triangle coercion faithfully renders garbage-in. THE REAL BLOCKER is the SERVER capture: the isSpritePara raw-order fix (rc.verts[pp.first..+4] direct) is committed (6b28bac21) + built (md5 1a07ccba, source:hud_engineorder_staged_binary) but NOT DEPLOYED to prod. Until the rebuilt headless binary ships, every fresh prod capture still feeds the client the rc.idx-corrupted leading-letter sprites, so the names garble REGARDLESS of any client triangulation change. RECONCILED ONE TRUTH: triangulation = FAITHFUL (this finding); strip order = identity [0,1,2,3] (=engine\'s own tris); decode = correct RGB565/PAL4 twiddle; the OUTSTANDING root cause = the un-deployed server capture fix. No client render-method change is warranted.',
  status='confirmed', confidence='high', date='2026-06-15';

UPSERT finding:hud_sprite_no_native_render_needed SET
  statement='TASK 2 (IF NOT FAITHFUL -> native sprite render): NOT TRIGGERED. Because the 2-triangle coercion IS faithful (finding:hud_sprite_2tri_coercion_is_faithful), the client does NOT need a native single-plane sprite renderer in pvr2-renderer.mjs/ta-parser.mjs. The correct client recipe is the one ALREADY shipped (finding:hud_sprite_engine_order_replaces_heuristic): for a captured ParaType-5 quad, emit the 4 corners in raw expansion order [P,C,A,B] as an identity triangle strip [0,1,2,3], each vertex keeping its CAPTURED u/v/col (P keeps its plane-interpolated UV) — pvr2 _buildIndexBuffer draws (P,C,A)+(A,C,B), byte-identical to flycast. REQUIRED INPUT DATA: the 4 EXPANDED verts + their captured UVs (incl P\'s plane-interpolated UV) — NOT the 3 original TA_Sprite corners. Rationale: the engine\'s plane solve is the source of P\'s UV, and flycast itself rasterizes from the 4 expanded verts, so capturing post-expansion [P,C,A,B] (which collectHudQuads already does, once isSpritePara reads rc.verts directly) gives the client the exact same primitive flycast draws — no need to re-implement CaclulateSpritePlane client-side, no need to capture the pre-expansion 3 corners. The ONLY change still needed is on the SERVER (deploy the isSpritePara binary), after which the existing client identity-strip path renders the full names with zero further work.',
  status='confirmed', confidence='high', date='2026-06-15';

UPSERT finding:hud_sprite_capture_call_order_sane SET
  statement='TASK 3 (server-capture call-order sanity): the deployed isSpritePara path captures the sprite verts in RAW expansion order [P,C,A,B], and autosort does NOT mutate rc.verts/pp.first out from under it — VERIFIED from source. (1) WHAT collectHudQuads READS (maplecast_oracle_hook.cpp:3025-3047): for isSpritePara (((pcw>>29)&7)==5) the primary rc.idx walk is SKIPPED (the `if(!isSpritePara)` guard) so seen stays 0, and the `if(seen==0)` branch reads rc.verts[pp.first .. pp.first+pp.count] DIRECTLY into vidx[0..3]. (2) WHY THAT IS RAW ORDER: ta_vtx.cpp AppendSpriteVertexA does vd_rc.verts.resize(+4) and writes cv[0..3]=[P,C,A,B] CONTIGUOUSLY at the slot pp.first recorded in AppendSpriteParam (d_pp->first = vd_rc.verts.size() BEFORE the resize). Those 4 verts are NEVER reordered. (3) DOES AUTOSORT TOUCH THEM? sortTriangles (ta_util.cpp:66-185) reads ctx.verts[pp->first..] read-only (L88-106), builds a NEW triangleList, std::stable_sorts THAT list (L133), and APPENDS depth-sorted indices to ctx.idx (L156-158) starting at idxSize — it does NOT modify ctx.verts, does NOT modify pp->first, does NOT reorder the contiguous vert run. So even after autosort runs, rc.verts[pp.first..+4] still holds [P,C,A,B] in raw order; only rc.idx is permuted (which is exactly why the OLD rc.idx-walk capture was corrupt, finding:hud_sprite_capture_server_bug, and why the isSpritePara path bypasses rc.idx). (4) CALL ORDER: collectHudQuads is invoked via mc_oracle_collectHud(rcv) on the surviving HUD render context AFTER the TA list is finalized (autosort may already have run for the tr list); because the sprite path reads rc.verts-direct (autosort-immune), the capture is correct whether autosort ran or not. CONCLUSION: the deployed isSpritePara path DOES capture raw [P,C,A,B] order; autosort\'s sortTriangles cannot corrupt it because it only touches rc.idx, never rc.verts or pp.first. The capture is sound; the remaining gap is purely DEPLOYMENT of that binary to prod.',
  status='confirmed', confidence='high', date='2026-06-15';

RELATE finding:hud_sprite_2tri_coercion_is_faithful->cites->source:hud_sprite_raster_path_flycast;
RELATE finding:hud_sprite_2tri_coercion_is_faithful->cites->source:hud_sprite_expand_engine_order;
RELATE finding:hud_sprite_2tri_coercion_is_faithful->about->finding:hud_sprite_engine_order_replaces_heuristic;
RELATE finding:hud_sprite_2tri_coercion_is_faithful->about->finding:hud_sprite_capture_server_bug;
RELATE finding:hud_sprite_2tri_coercion_is_faithful->about->finding:hud_name_garble_is_paratype5_sprite;
RELATE finding:hud_sprite_no_native_render_needed->cites->source:hud_sprite_raster_path_flycast;
RELATE finding:hud_sprite_no_native_render_needed->about->finding:hud_sprite_2tri_coercion_is_faithful;
RELATE finding:hud_sprite_no_native_render_needed->about->finding:hud_sprite_engine_order_replaces_heuristic;
RELATE finding:hud_sprite_capture_call_order_sane->cites->source:hud_sprite_raster_path_flycast;
RELATE finding:hud_sprite_capture_call_order_sane->cites->source:hud_engineorder_staged_binary;
RELATE finding:hud_sprite_capture_call_order_sane->about->finding:hud_sprite_capture_server_bug;

-- ============================================================================
-- 36j (2026-06-15): THE fmt=5 PAL4 SPRITE-TEXTURE DECODE, GROUNDED IN FLYCAST
-- texconv + LIVE VRAM. Task: prove/fix the texture-manager._pal4 decode for the
-- HUD PAL4 glyphs (tcw 2a082c00). VERDICT: _pal4 is BYTE-IDENTICAL to flycast's
-- ConvertTwiddlePal4 (0/1024 nibble diff, proven two independent ways) AND decodes
-- the SIBLING PAL4 HUD sprites in the SAME live capture to CLEAN digits/gauges.
-- So there is NO PAL4 decode bug. The one noisy sprite (0x416000 super-art) is a
-- SERVER VRAM-PREFIX TIMING gap (art loaded AFTER the prefix snapshot), the twin
-- of finding:hud_meter_segments_vram_gap. The validation GATE is the user's
-- browser (_hudRenderMode), not the Dawn harness. feat/render-replica-live.
-- ============================================================================

UPSERT source:hud_pal4_decode_flycast SET kind='code',
  ref='core/rend/texconv.cpp ConvertTwiddlePal4 (L288-318), texture_TW (L385-406, p=&p_in[(twop(x,y,bcx,bcy)/divider)<<3], divider=xpp*ypp=16), UnpackerPalToRgb (L278-286, pal[col] @ palette_index), palette_update (L81-149: PAL_RAM_CTRL&3 -> [1555,565,4444,8888]); core/rend/TexCache.cpp Update (L463-472: PixelPal4 -> ::palette_index = tcw.PalSelect<<4) + L320-396 (width=8<<tsp.TexU, height=8<<tsp.TexV, twiddled NON-mip keeps TexV, size=w*h*bpp/8, mmStartAddress=startAddress); core/hw/pvr/pvr_regs.h (PAL_RAM_CTRL_addr 0x108, PALETTE_RAM_START_addr 0x1000). Client under test: web/webgpu/texture-manager.mjs _pal4 + updatePalette + getTexture.',
  note='THE AUTHORITATIVE PAL4 DECODE (flycast) vs the client _pal4. (a) DIMS: from TSP, w=8<<((tsp>>3)&7), h=8<<(tsp&7); for a twiddled NON-mip PAL4 flycast keeps TexV (height=8<<TexV, NOT forced square — square-force is mip-only, TexCache.cpp L371-373). (b) TWIDDLE/NIBBLE ORDER: texture_TW walks 4x4 blocks (xpp=ypp=4); block source byte = (twop(blockX,blockY)/16)<<3 = 8 contiguous bytes = 16 nibbles; ConvertTwiddlePal4 distributes those 16 nibbles into the block in Morton intra-block order (0,0)(0,1)(1,0)(1,1)(0,2)(0,3)(1,2)(1,3)(2,0)(2,1)(3,0)(3,1)(2,2)(2,3)(3,2)(3,3), LOW nibble then HIGH nibble per byte. This is EQUIVALENT to the client _pal4 per-pixel inverse ti=twop(x,y), byte=ti>>1, nibble=ti&1: I built the flycast block-sequential map AND the _pal4 per-pixel map for 32x32 and they MATCH 0/1024 (twiddle index == nibble stream index for square PAL4; low-nibble=even ti). (c) PALETTE BANK: PixelPal4 -> palette_index = tcw.PalSelect<<4 (TexCache.cpp L466); client pb=palSel<<4 IDENTICAL; palSel=(tcw>>21)&0x3F=16 -> idx 256..271. (d) PALETTE ENTRY FMT: PAL_RAM_CTRL&3 -> case0 1555 / case1 565 / case2 4444 / case3 8888; live PAL_RAM_CTRL=2 (4444); client updatePalette uses [u1555,u565,u4444,(u4444|8888 via ctrl===3 branch)] IDENTICAL. EVERY field of the client decode matches flycast.';

UPSERT source:hud_pal4_live_vram_decode_2026_06_15 SET kind='oracle',
  ref='tools/render-replica-poc/_hud_cap_fresh3/{vram_prefix.bin 8MB, pvr_prefix.bin 32KB, hudq_inventory.txt 61 quads vframe 882168}. Decode scripts (local /tmp, ran via node): flycast-literal ConvertTwiddlePal4 vs client _pal4 nibble diff; ASCII-alpha decode of each fmt=5 sprite at its TSP dims. Live PAL_RAM_CTRL=2, palette bank16 populated (15/16 ARGB4444 non-zero).',
  note='LIVE-VRAM DECODE OF THE fmt=5 PAL4 HUD SPRITES (the verbatim client _pal4, real prod VRAM+palette). The 6 fmt=5 sprites in the capture are ALL tsp=949004d2 (32x32), tcw 2a08xx / 2b08xx, palSel 16/24, twiddled, non-VQ, non-mip. RESULT decoding each at 32x32 with the EXACT client _pal4: q44 0x418800, q45 0x418a00, q46 0x418c00, q47 0x41a800, q48 0x41aa00 (the super-meter level digit/gauge cells) decode to CLEAN structured glyphs (framed digit boxes, ### notches, gauge segments — visibly correct in _RENDER_all.png bottom-right as a readable purple/yellow figure + digit cells). ONLY q41 0x416000 + q42 0x416400 (the bottom-CENTER super-move character art, screen 53x69 @ cy=435, full UVs) decode to COLOUR-NOISE at 32x32 AND at 64x64/128x128/32x64 AND as RGB565 — i.e. the bytes at 0x416000 are not a coherent texture in ANY format/size. SAME _pal4 code, SAME tcw format, SAME palette: clean for 0x418xxx, noise for 0x416000 => the divergence is the VRAM DATA at 0x416000, not the decode. The 128x128 decode of 0x416000 shows SCATTERED face/sprite FRAGMENTS (a stale/aliased different texture), confirming wrong-data not wrong-decode.';

UPSERT finding:hud_pal4_decode_is_correct_residual_is_vram_timing SET
  statement='THE fmt=5 PAL4 SPRITE-TEXTURE DECODE IS CORRECT; there is NO _pal4 bug. PROVEN: (1) web/webgpu/texture-manager.mjs _pal4 is BYTE-IDENTICAL to flycast core/rend/texconv.cpp ConvertTwiddlePal4 (source:hud_pal4_decode_flycast) — a literal port of flycast\'s block-sequential ConvertTwiddlePal4 (texture_TW p=&p_in[(twop/16)<<3], 16-nibble Morton intra-block, low-then-high nibble) vs the client\'s per-pixel inverse (ti=twop(x,y); byte=ti>>1; nibble=ti&1) produce IDENTICAL nibble streams for the 32x32 sprite (0/1024 diff). Every field matches flycast: DIMS w=8<<TexU/h=8<<TexV from TSP (twiddled non-mip keeps TexV, no square-force), palette bank = tcw.PalSelect<<4 (=256 for palSel16), palette entry fmt from PAL_RAM_CTRL&3 (=2 -> ARGB4444), index0=transparent. (2) The SAME _pal4 decodes the SIBLING fmt=5 PAL4 HUD sprites in the SAME live capture (0x418800/0x418c00/0x41a800, the super-meter level/digit/gauge cells) to CLEAN readable glyphs (source:hud_pal4_live_vram_decode_2026_06_15). So the decode path WORKS on real PAL4 sprite data. (3) ONLY the bottom-center super-move character art at VRAM 0x416000/0x416400 (tcw 2a082c00, q41/q42, cy=435 53x69) decodes to noise — and it decodes to noise in EVERY format/dimension (PAL4 32/64/128, 565), with the 128x128 view showing scattered stale face fragments => the bytes at 0x416000 in the shipped prefix are a STALE/ALIASED different texture, not this frame\'s super-art. ROOT CAUSE = SERVER VRAM-PREFIX TIMING GAP (the twin of finding:hud_meter_segments_vram_gap): per maplecast_replica_live.cpp the 8MB VRAM prefix is snapshotted ONCE (lazy, first armed in-match frame) and any texture written to VRAM AFTER that snapshot is FROZEN at the stale value (cpp comment L219-224: "Any bank written AFTER the prefix was cached is FROZEN at the snapshot value... timing-dependent, not range"). The super-MOVE art (a hyper-combo / special portrait) is loaded into VRAM at move-time, AFTER the prefix snapshot, so 0x416000 carries whatever occupied it at snapshot time; the meter-digit cells (0x418xxx) load at match-start, before the snapshot, so they are correct. FIX DOMAIN = SERVER, not the client _pal4: re-snapshot VRAM (or ship a per-frame dirty-page VRAM tail covering the super-art banks) when the super/special art is resident, identical remedy class to finding:hud_meter_segments_vram_gap. The client _pal4 needs NO change and must NOT be "fixed" — altering it would regress the cleanly-decoding 0x418xxx meter sprites. SUPERSEDES the task premise (and the title of re_kb 36 finding:hud_name_garble_is_texture_decode_not_order\'s implied "client decode bug"): the decode is byte-exact to flycast; the residual noise is bad INPUT DATA from a stale VRAM prefix bank. NOTE: the per-character BIG-NAME plates (STORM/SENTINEL/COLOSSUS/CABLE) the user reads in the gate render via the fmt=1 RGB565 FONT-frame quads (clean) + already-resident art, NOT these post-snapshot super sprites; with _hudRenderMode=no-sprites the HUD is fully clean, confirming the only residual is the post-snapshot PAL4 super-art VRAM, a server gap.',
  status='confirmed', confidence='high', date='2026-06-15';

RELATE finding:hud_pal4_decode_is_correct_residual_is_vram_timing->cites->source:hud_pal4_decode_flycast;
RELATE finding:hud_pal4_decode_is_correct_residual_is_vram_timing->cites->source:hud_pal4_live_vram_decode_2026_06_15;
RELATE finding:hud_pal4_decode_is_correct_residual_is_vram_timing->about->finding:hud_name_garble_is_texture_decode_not_order;
RELATE finding:hud_pal4_decode_is_correct_residual_is_vram_timing->about->finding:hud_meter_segments_vram_gap;
RELATE finding:hud_pal4_decode_is_correct_residual_is_vram_timing->supersedes->finding:hud_name_garble_is_texture_decode_not_order;

-- ============================================================================
-- 36k (2026-06-15): THE "HUD TEXTURE Y-ORIENTATION BUG" (name/time/font glyphs
-- decode UPSIDE-DOWN, portraits decode UPRIGHT, same _decode) IS A PROBE ARTIFACT,
-- NOT a decode bug. twop/detwiddle is BYTE-IDENTICAL to flycast texconv.cpp; the
-- font atlas is STORED upside-down in VRAM and the engine flips it via per-quad UVs.
-- A global decode V-flip fixes the names BUT breaks the portraits = WRONG fix.
-- The fix is a UV-AWARE PROBE, zero change to the decode that drives the real render.
-- feat/render-replica-live. (mvc2-sprite-render expert.)
-- ============================================================================

UPSERT source:hud_twiddle_flycast_byteexact SET kind='code',
  ref='web/webgpu/texture-manager.mjs tw()+detwiddle build (L4-14 region) + twop + _decode (idx = scan===1 ? y*w+x : twop(x,y,bx,by)) vs core/rend/texconv.cpp twiddle_slow (L37-66) + detwiddle table build (L69-79) + twop (L169-171). web/render-replica/replay.html buildHudTA (q.u[i]/q.v[i] passthrough at the dv.setFloat32(o+16/o+20) lines). Live capture: tools/render-replica-poc/_hud_cap_fresh3/hudq_inventory.txt (vframe 882168, 61 quads) + _RENDER_all.png.',
  note='LINE-BY-LINE TWIDDLE DIVERGENCE CHECK (the task hypothesis: "our twop is Y-inverted vs flycast" — REFUTED). (1) tw(x,y,xs,ys) does xs>>=1;ys>>=1; then per loop iteration emits the Y bit FIRST (if(ys){r|=(y&1)<<s;...s++}) then the X bit (if(xs){r|=(x&1)<<s;...s++}) — IDENTICAL bit order to flycast twiddle_slow (Y branch before X branch). (2) Table build: detwiddle[0][s][i]=tw(i,0,1024,1<<s); detwiddle[1][s][i]=tw(0,i,1<<s,1024) — IDENTICAL args to flycast detwiddle[0][s][i]=twiddle_slow(i,0,x_sz=1024,y_sz=1<<s); detwiddle[1][s][i]=twiddle_slow(0,i,1<<s,1024). (3) twop(x,y,bx,by)=detwiddle[0][by][x]+detwiddle[1][bx][y] — IDENTICAL. So there is NO Y-inversion in the twiddle and NO per-format orientation in _decode (the linear path is y*w+x, also no flip). (4) THE PROBE EVIDENCE EXPLAINED BY DATA, NOT DECODE: in _hud_cap_fresh3 the FONT/digit quads (tcw 0809e1/e2/e3/e7/e8/e9, e.g. quad[35] y=[62,62,71,71] v=[0.500,0.500,0.250,0.250]) have v DECREASING as screen-y INCREASES => the engine V-FLIPS the font atlas via UVs (the atlas is stored bottom-up). The portrait/icon quads (tcw 0809de/df/e0, e.g. quad[27] y=[111,94,111,94] v=[0.016,0.984,0.016,0.984]) map top screen-y->high v with the atlas stored upright. buildHudTA writes q.v[i] straight through, so the REAL render is upright for BOTH families (confirmed in _RENDER_all.png: "TRM"/"INEL"/"3LE" name letters + both portraits render UPRIGHT; the only garble is the bottom-center stale-VRAM PAL4 super-art, finding:hud_pal4_decode_is_correct_residual_is_vram_timing). The probe`s old `orig` thumbnail showed the RAW atlas (no UVs) => the bottom-up font atlas looked upside-down (EXPECTED) while the upright portrait atlas looked upright — a probe display artifact, not a render bug.';

UPSERT finding:hud_yorientation_is_vram_storage_not_decode SET
  statement='THE HUD TEXTURE Y-ORIENTATION "BUG" IS A PROBE ARTIFACT + VRAM STORAGE ORIENTATION, NOT a decode bug, and the correct fix is NOT a global/per-format decode V-flip. ROOT CAUSE: the FONT/digit/name glyph atlas (tcw 0809BE00 / 0809Exx, fmt=1 RGB565 twiddled, VRAM 0x4DF000/0x4F0xxx) is STORED UPSIDE-DOWN (bottom-up) in VRAM and the engine flips it UPRIGHT on screen via each quad`s captured UVs (digit quad: top screen-y maps to a HIGHER v; source:hud_twiddle_flycast_byteexact, _hud_cap_fresh3 quad[35] v=[0.5,0.5,0.25,0.25] over y=[62,62,71,71]); the portrait/icon atlas (tcw 0809DE/DF/E0) is STORED UPRIGHT and its UVs do not flip. The client decode is byte-exact to flycast: texture-manager.mjs tw()/detwiddle/twop are LINE-IDENTICAL to texconv.cpp twiddle_slow/detwiddle/twop (no Y-inversion), and _decode`s linear path is y*w+x (no flip). buildHudTA (replay.html) passes q.u[i]/q.v[i] through verbatim, so the REAL on-screen HUD render is already UPRIGHT for BOTH names AND portraits (PROVEN: _hud_cap_fresh3/_RENDER_all.png shows the name letters "TRM"/"INEL"/"3LE" and both portraits upright through the verbatim pvr2 path). WHY THE GLOBAL FLIP IS WRONG (the user`s observation, now explained): a global decode V-flip makes the RAW font atlas read upright in the probe AND fixes nothing on screen except by DOUBLE-FLIPPING — it would (a) double-flip the names (UVs already flip them) turning them upside-down on screen, and (b) flip the already-upright portraits upside-down. The names "looked fixed" by flipV only in the no-UV RAW probe thumbnail, which is not what the canvas draws. THE FIX (client, probe only): make __probeHudNames render an `orig (UV)` thumbnail that samples each texture THROUGH that quad`s captured UVs (bilinear across the screen-ordered corners) = exactly how buildHudTA/pvr2 draws it; this shows the FONT names AND the portraits BOTH UPRIGHT at `orig (UV)`, proving the decode + UV path are correct, while a `raw`/`raw·flipV` pair stays for diagnostics. ZERO change to _decode/twop (a PROTECTED comment now guards them against a future global-flip regression). Deployed in replay.html __probeHudNames (mkUV), texture-manager import bumped ?v=scene5, title tag hud:ui2-yorient-uvprobe. RECONCILES the task premise: there is no twop Y-inversion; the names-vs-portraits orientation split is the VRAM storage orientation the engine compensates per-quad via UVs.',
  status='confirmed', confidence='high', date='2026-06-15';

RELATE finding:hud_yorientation_is_vram_storage_not_decode->cites->source:hud_twiddle_flycast_byteexact;
RELATE finding:hud_yorientation_is_vram_storage_not_decode->cites->source:hud_pal4_decode_flycast;
RELATE finding:hud_yorientation_is_vram_storage_not_decode->about->finding:hud_pal4_decode_is_correct_residual_is_vram_timing;
RELATE finding:hud_yorientation_is_vram_storage_not_decode->about->finding:hud_garble_one_truth_clientgrounded;
